# Illustration colours

Use this page to choose colours for an illustration, such as a picture that explains where to put a sensor. For when to use an illustration and how to draw it, see [Illustration](illustration.html). Illustration colours do not change with the household's theme colour.

## Illustrations palette

Use the palette for the channel where the illustration will appear. For example, use [Web colours](colour-web.html) for documentation pages and dashboards, and [Social colours](colour-social.html) for social media.

Use as few colours as you can. As a rule of thumb, use 4 to 6 colours, depending on how complex the illustration is and how many elements it has.

<%= colour_copy_status %>

<%= colour_palette('illustrations', fields: %i[hex rgb]) %>

Primary blue is the base of the brand, so use it heavily in illustrations. Use Accent teal and Accent blue if they suit the illustration and its layout. They are bold, so use them to highlight a key component.

<%= example('illustration-colour-applying') %>

### Colour pairing

Choose colours that complement each other, with enough contrast to tell the key elements apart and keep a clear visual hierarchy.

Use different shades of one colour to separate the foreground from the background, so that the image does not look flat.

<%= example('illustration-colour-pairing') %>

### Using contrast

Contrasting colours make an illustration more appealing and easier to see.

Test the illustration with a colour blindness simulator, to check that people with a colour vision deficiency do not lose important information.

A warm colour beside a cool one, such as yellow beside blue, makes a focal point that draws attention to the action. Keep Primary blue dominant.

<%= example('illustration-colour-contrast') %>

## Maintenance

GOV.UK values live in `docs/data/govuk-brand-colours.json`, under `pages.illustrations`. The example illustrations are generated from that file by `docs/scripts/generate-illustration-examples.py`. If a colour changes, run the script again rather than editing the illustrations by hand; `--check` reports illustrations that are out of date. Run `npm run check:colours` after the docs build.
